//? Title: Compare several group means with MANOVA
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Compare groups on two outcomes together

Three settings are tested on independent greenhouse trays. Each tray
produces two scores, yield and growth. MANOVA compares the group mean
vectors. The hypothesis matrix measures separation among the group means,
and the error matrix measures variation around those means. The four
criteria summarize the same generalized roots of the hypothesis matrix
relative to the error matrix.

```{cpp, id=one-way-manova, file="figures/manova_group_scores.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/manova.hpp>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <numeric>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge;
    linalg::DenseMatrix<double> response(18,2);
    std::vector<std::int64_t> groups;
    std::array<std::vector<double>,3> yield, growth;
    for (std::size_t row = 0; row < response.rows(); ++row) {
        const double i = row + 1;
        const std::size_t group = row < 5 ? 0 : (row < 11 ? 1 : 2);
        const auto variation = std::sin(.37*i);
        response(row,0) = 3+group+.45*variation+.4*std::cos(1.8*i);
        response(row,1) = 6+.5*group-.5*variation+.55*std::sin(1.4*i);
        groups.push_back(group);
        yield[group].push_back(response(row,0));
        growth[group].push_back(response(row,1));
    }

    const auto result = stats::one_way_manova(response,groups);
    std::println("| Criterion | Value | F | Numerator df | Denominator df | p-value |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    const auto show = [](const char* name, const stats::MANOVACriterion& criterion) {
        const auto& f = criterion.reference.value();
        std::println("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.3f} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.3g} |", name, criterion.statistic,
            f.statistic,f.df_numerator,f.df_denominator,f.p_value);
    };
    show("Pillai",result.pillai);
    show("Wilks",result.wilks);
    show("Hotelling–Lawley",result.hotelling_lawley);
    show("Roy",result.roy);

    auto plot = graphics::RPlot::create();
    const std::array colors{graphics::RGBA{23,85,146},graphics::RGBA{161,75,37},graphics::RGBA{31,117,99}};
    for (std::size_t group = 0; group < colors.size(); ++group) {
        const auto mean = [](const std::vector<double>& values) {
            return std::accumulate(values.begin(),values.end(),0.0)/values.size();
        };
        auto light = colors[group];
        light.alpha = .55;
        plot.points(yield[group],growth[group],light,4)
            .points({mean(yield[group])},{mean(growth[group])},
                "Setting " + std::to_string(group+1),colors[group],7);
    }
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    plot.title("Yield and growth across three settings")
        .x_label("Yield score").y_label("Growth score").y_limits(4.75,8.65)
        .size(720,450).save_svg("build/manova_group_scores.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Criterion | Value | F | Numerator df | Denominator df | p-value |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Pillai | 0.8924 | 6.042 | 4.00 | 30.00 | 0.00109 |
| Wilks | 0.1152 | 13.626 | 4.00 | 28.00 | 2.76e-06 |
| Hotelling–Lawley | 7.6164 | 24.753 | 4.00 | 26.00 | 1.54e-08 |
| Roy | 7.6078 | 57.059 | 2.00 | 15.00 | 9.73e-08 |
\image(figures/manova_group_scores.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Small points show individual trays. The larger point of each color shows its setting's mean vector.)
\alttext(The three group means rise from left to right. Setting 1 has the lowest yield and growth means, and Setting 3 has the highest.)

A large root means the group means are spread out along an outcome
combination with relatively little within-group variation. All four
criteria give evidence that the mean vectors differ in this example.
Their F reference distributions use different approximations, with
Roy's F serving as an upper bound for a multi-root effect.

[Pillai, Some New Test Criteria in Multivariate Analysis|https://doi.org/10.1214/aoms/1177728599]
